179	9	2	Ethical vs. Cost-Based Routing in Logistics: A Comparative Analysis	Multi-Source Reasoning	{"Passage": "Memo from the CEO of GreenLogistics Inc.: As we review last quarter's logistical decisions, I emphasize the need to balance cost efficiency with ethical responsibility. Data indicates that sustainable routing, while initially more expensive, leads to significant reductions in emissions and enhances brand reputation over time.\\nEmail from Logistics Manager to the Planning Team: Following the CEO's directive, I've analyzed Q4 data. Ethical routing increased costs by 10% but cut emissions by 25% compared to cost-focused options. However, this aligns with our guidelines, as the emission reduction per $1,000 is 6.25 tons, exceeding the 5-ton threshold. Should we adjust future plans to prioritize ethics more consistently?", "Source1": {"para": "GreenLogistics Inc. faces an ethical dilemma in planning a major shipment: Route A costs $50,000 with 200 tons of CO2 emissions, while Route B costs $60,000 but emits only 150 tons. Company guidelines state that ethical routing is mandatory if the emission reduction per $1,000 cost increase exceeds 5 tons. Last quarter, 60% of similar decisions favored cost over ethics, despite a 25% boost in brand loyalty for sustainable choices."}, "Source2": {"table": {"type": "sortable_table", "structure": {"rows": [["Q1", "55", "210", "30", "65"], ["Q2", "53", "205", "35", "68"], ["Q3", "52", "200", "38", "72"], ["Q4", "50", "195", "40", "75"]], "title": "Quarterly Logistical Performance Trends", "headers": ["Quarter", "Average Cost per Shipment ($000)", "Average Emissions per Shipment (tons)", "Ethical Decision Rate (%)", "Customer Loyalty Score"], "formatting": {"sortable": true, "alternating_rows": true}}, "description": "Table showing quarterly trends in logistical performance metrics for GreenLogistics Inc., including cost, emissions, ethical decisions, and customer loyalty, to support trend analysis in ethical planning."}}, "S