2702	10	1	LCM of Pathogen Replication Intervals in Epidemiology	Information: In an epidemiology study, two pathogens replicate every m days and n days, with m and n positive integers.\nQuantity A: The number of days until both replicate on the same day.\nQuantity B: m * n	4	0	0	{}	2025-12-29 04:32:22.257	2025-12-29 04:32:22.257	f	\N	f	{"explanation": "**Setup:** Quantity A is LCM(m,n), Quantity B is m*n.\\n**Execution:** $LCM(m,n) = (m \\\\cdot n) / GCD(m,n)$. Since $GCD(m,n) \\\\geq 1$, LCM ≤ m*n, equal if GCD=1.\\n**Verification:** m,n are positive integers; LCM can equal or be less than m*n, so relationship is indeterminate.\\n**Distractors:** Assuming A < B always is wrong as m,n could be coprime."}	\N	\N	single	0	[Topic: Number Properties and Fractions, Decimals, Percentages] | [Theme: Healthcare Analytics & Epidemiology] | [Style: <Numeric Comparisons>] | [Difficulty: <4>]	Quantitative Comparison
2703	10	1	Philosophy & Ethics: Mutually Exclusive Probability Question	An ethical dilemma involves two mutually exclusive options. Option X has a probability of 0.4 of being ethical, and Option Y has 0.3. What is the probability that neither option is ethical?	1	0	0	{}	2025-12-29 04:32:22.301	2025-12-29 04:32:22.301	f	\N	f	{"explanation": "**Step 1: Setup** Events X and Y are **mutually exclusive**, so $P(X \\\\text{ and } Y) = 0$ with $P(X)=0.4$ and $P(Y)=0.3$.\\n**Step 2: Execution** $P(\\\\text{neither}) = 1 - P(X \\\\text{ or } Y) = 1 - [P(X) + P(Y)] = 1 - (0.4 + 0.3) = 0.3$.\\n**Step 3: Verification** Total probability sums to 1: $0.4 + 0.3 + 0.3 = 1.0$, confirming correctness.\\n**Step 4: Distractors** A common error is assuming independence and computing $(1-0.4)(1-0.3)=0.42$, which is wrong due to mutual exclusivity."}	\N	\N	single	0	[Topic: Probability Rules (Basic, Independent, Mutually Exclusive, etc)] | [Theme: Philosophy & Ethics] | [Style: <Inequalities>] | [Difficulty: <1>]	Problem Solving Simple
